# A double-width line holds half the screen's columns, so a right-to-left
# paragraph on it is drawn inside that half. SCP 2 makes the paragraph
# right to left, and ESC # 6 makes the line double width. On a screen 10
# columns wide the first line shows 5 cells: its 3 trailing erased cells
# at the left, then ab at the half's right edge. The second line mixes a
# Hebrew alef with a Latin b, and is drawn b then alef, also against the
# half's right edge. The third line is single width and shows all 10
# cells, so ab stands at the screen's right edge. SCP 1 on the fourth
# line, at its first column, makes that paragraph left to right, so the
# cursor stays at the left of an empty row.
# spec: ADR-1004 D8, "Double-size rows": "A double-width or double-height row reorders as any row does, over its half as many cells."; ADR-1004 D3: "Rules X1 to I2 run over the whole paragraph, and rules L1 and L2 run a row at a time, so text never moves from one row to another."; ADR-1004 D3: "The erased cells after a row's last written cell take no part in the algorithm, and they are drawn at the row's end in the paragraph's direction: at the right in a left-to-right paragraph and at the left in a right-to-left one, as the recommendation's chapter on erased cells says."; the VT100 User Guide, DECDWL: "The use of double-width characters reduces the number of characters per line by half"
# covers: text.bidi
size 10 4
send "\e[2 k\e#6ab\r\n\e#6\u{5D0}b\r\nab\r\n\e[1 k"
dump
